pokemon  you've gotta catch em all as the old  slogan goes  but what do you do once you have caught  them all  explore other interests  better yourself through learning  don't be ridiculous what you should do  is play other games that are sort of  similar to pokemon  and boy do we have you covered on that  front with a wide array of games that  hit the spot for pokemon fans  not all of these recommendations follow  the pokemon formula but they all capture  some piece of what makes pokemon such an  enduring sensation  check out our list and shout i choose  you  [Music]  coramon coramon is decidedly retro with  a pixel-based art style that looks to  emulate the earliest days of the pokemon  series it's one of the most faithful  pokemon likes around letting you collect  train and battle more than 100 creatures  with elemental skills with that  framework it does have some unique  twists like a stamina system that  defines your combat actions and a  flexible difficulty system that lets you  customize the game to your own comfort  level this includes an easy mode that  lets you just experience the story and  even a randomizer feature to mix up your  experience if you like the core ideas of  pokemon but want to see it rift on this  is a good pick  chocobo mystery dungeon everybody  this cutesy classically styled roguelike  starring the final fantasy mascot may  not immediately seem pokemon light and  in many ways it isn't but what it lacks  in specific mechanics it makes up for in  that same collecting spirit as chocobo  explores dungeons they'll find tons of  other notable final fantasy monsters and  creatures and bring them aboard as  buddies hence the name everybody half  the fun of pokemon is finding the  variety of creatures to recruit into  your party and chocobo mystery dungeon  everybody scratches that same itch with  the familiar faces of final fantasy  [Music]  nino cooney wrath of the white witch  nino kuni has become a franchise in its  own right but whereas the sequel and  mobile game are more styled as action  rpgs the first game released in the west  is very clearly inspired by the pokemon  series the combat system revolves around  capturing and taming a host of creatures  called familiars and then using them to  battle with other creatures while the  monster designs are not quite as  imaginative and varied as pokemon the  studio ghibli inspired art style and  dreamlike story are a captivating change  of pace and while the languid pace of  the game may have been hard to play on  its original playstation 3 release it's  been ported to just about everything  including nintendo switch just in case  you prefer your pokemon likes to be  portable  ooblets  ubletz is not entirely pokemon like  given its eccentric mixture of genres  and influences but somewhere in this  quirky blend is definitely some pokemon  dna in oublets you capture and do battle  with a variety of little captured  creatures the pool is smaller than a  pokemon game but they're differentiated  well and the game isn't intending to  blow you away with sheer variety instead  each one feels like a sweet little cozy  creation with names like dumburb and  wiggle whip  and rather than a typical one-on-one  pokemon battle  your creatures take part in non-violent  dance battles  that sense of infectious goofiness  permeates the whole experience giving it  a friendly feel and once you've captured  your creatures you can assign them to  help with small tasks or to tend to your  growing garden where you harvest food  for sale or other ooblets to add to your  collection  [Music]  monster crown monster crown is another  relatively faithful pokemon like game  but it's made for pokemon fans who have  grown into adults the story and creature  designs are darker than a traditional  pokemon game and the cute pixelated art  belies some fairly serious themes the  traditional monster collecting hooks are  still there though with more than 200  creatures to collect and evolve you can  even battle and trade online  [Music]  monster hunter stories and stories too  the monster hunter series isn't very  pokemon like at all considering in  pokemon they aren't known for skinning  and wearing the creatures  the more family-friendly spin-off  monster hunter stories though is much  more about raising and forming bonds  with the creatures in a more traditional  rpg structure mechanically the battle  system works very differently than a  traditional pokemon game with a slightly  more action-oriented structure and  different elemental structure for  weaknesses but it scratches the itch of  collecting monsters especially if you  have an affinity for monster hunter  designs who doesn't want to befriend a  rathalos both monster hunter stories  games are solid but monster hunter  stories 2 is better in almost every way  and doesn't require much knowledge of  the first game  [Music]  monster sanctuary  and now for something completely  different  monster sanctuary doesn't resemble  pokemon mechanically at all it's much  more of a traditional platforming action  side scroller but it does fulfill the  fantasy of taming wild beasts and using  them to traverse the world in a way  that's much more active than pokemon as  you build your array of beasts and level  them up you can use them in combat to  reach new heights and to ride on it's as  if the core concepts of pokemon were  interpreted in a completely different  way which is a very neat trick if you're  up for something unique  [Music]  nexomon and nexomon extinction  nexomon is another traditional pokemon  like with a bright colorful art style  and a huge wealth of 300 or so monsters  to collect and battle  this one mixes in some light resource  collecting and crafting with elemental  shards and the art style is very anime  inspired nexum on extinction the more  recent sequel throws you right into the  action with massive dragon tyrants  wreaking havoc but it's still very easy  to jump into the tamers guild and get  started and if you've always wanted more  starter options neximon makes a point of  giving you tons to choose from instead  of just the traditional fire grass water  trinity both games are available on  nintendo switch  [Music]  temtem  if you're at all interested in pokemon  likes you've almost definitely already  heard of temtem which has been in early  access since 2020. this is probably the  highest profile of all the games built  to take on the mega franchise indie  studio crema is aiming to build the  monster collecting mmo that pokemon fans  have been waiting for and all  indications from the early access  version are that it's pretty successful  at it now it's preparing for a full  version 1.0 launch in september for  consoles and pc and crema still has a  road map of continued support going  forward while lots of these games are  loving homages temtem is aiming to take  on the king  [Music]  world of final fantasy maxima  world of final fantasy is a cute chibi  rpg with a unique monster stacking  mechanic each character has its own size  class and you can manage the combat by  piling them on top of each other that  may not sound very pokemon like but like  chocobo's mystery dungeon square enix  makes the most of its long history of  iconic monster designs  you might do battle with a mughal  behemoth carbuncle and a little adorable  sephiroth all in different positions the  mechanic plays into the unique battle  system for something unlike anything  else but great for scratching that  collecting itch with creature designs  you already know and love  so there you have it an extensive list  of what to play when you can't play any  more pokemon i am a huge fan of studio  ghibli and definitely want to check out  nino cooney just to be able to virtually  inhabit one of their fantastic worlds  let us know in the comments what your  favorite non-pokemon pokemon game is  catch you next time here at gamespot  [Music]  you

